    {"q": "At what temperature is liquid water densest?", "options": ["0 °C", "About 4 °C", "About 20 °C", "100 °C"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["Water near 0 °C is actually slightly less dense than at 4 °C. That’s why it stays on top of a lake.", "Correct. That’s why the bottom of a deep lake tends to sit near 4 °C in winter.", "Warmer water is less dense than water at 4 °C.", "Near boiling, water is at its least dense as a liquid."]},
    {"q": "What holds ice in its open hexagonal structure?", "options": ["Magnetic forces", "Hydrogen bonds", "Air bubbles", "Salt crystals"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["Water isn’t magnetic in any way that matters here.", "Correct. Attractions between the slightly positive hydrogens and slightly negative oxygens lock the molecules at fixed distances.", "Bubbles can make ice cloudy, but bubble-free ice still floats.", "Pure ice contains no salt. Freezing actually pushes salt out."]},
    {"q": "About how much of an iceberg floating in seawater is below the surface?", "options": ["About 10%", "About 50%", "About 89%", "100%"], "answer": 2, "explain": ["That’s the part you can see, the “tip.”", "That would need ice to be half as dense as seawater.", "Correct. Ice is about 89% as dense as seawater, so about 89% sits underwater.", "If all of it were underwater, it would be sinking, not floating."]},
    {"q": "Why might people let a tap drip during a hard freeze?", "options": ["Dripping water heats the pipe", "Moving water is less likely to freeze and burst the pipe by expanding", "To reduce water pressure to zero", "It has no real effect"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["The drip doesn’t add meaningful heat.", "Correct. Flowing water resists freezing, and an open tap also eases pressure building between the ice and the tap.", "Pressure doesn’t go to zero, but an open tap does relieve the pressure that builds up.", "It does help, which is why plumbers recommend it."]}
