    {"q": "Why do central banks target about 2% instead of 0%?", "options": ["It’s required by global treaty", "To have a buffer against deflation and room to cut rates", "Because 0% is impossible to measure", "To raise government revenue"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["No treaty sets it. It’s a policy choice.", "Correct. A small positive rate guards against falling prices and keeps interest rates above zero, so they can be cut in a slump.", "Zero is measurable. It’s just risky to aim for.", "That isn’t the stated goal."]},
    {"q": "What did US Fed chair Paul Volcker do to end the 1970s inflation?", "options": ["Printed more money", "Raised interest rates to nearly 20%", "Froze all prices by law", "Abolished the dollar"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["That would worsen inflation.", "Correct. It caused a recession but broke the inflation spiral.", "Price controls were tried earlier, under Nixon, and failed.", "No."]}
