{
  "id": "math-birthday",
  "title": "The birthday paradox",
  "category": "math",
  "hook": "In a room of just 23 people, there’s a better than 50% chance that two share a birthday. With 70 people it’s 99.9%. Most people guess you’d need around 180. This isn’t a party trick: the same math tells cryptographers how secure a code is, and it shows why coincidences are far less surprising than they feel.",
  "sections": [
    {"heading": "Why intuition fails", "body": "When people hear “shared birthday,” they think about <em>their own</em> birthday matching someone else’s. That really is unlikely. You’d need about 253 other people for a 50% chance someone shares your specific day.\n\nBut the question is whether <strong>any two people</strong> match. With 23 people there are 23 × 22 ÷ 2 = <strong>253 pairs</strong>, and each pair is a chance for a match."},
    {"heading": "Calculate the opposite", "body": "It’s easier to compute the chance that <em>nobody</em> shares a birthday, then subtract from 1. Ignore leap days and assume all birthdays are equally likely.\n\nPerson 1 can have any day: 365/365. Person 2 must avoid that day: 364/365. Person 3 must avoid two days: 363/365. And so on. For 23 people:\n\n(365 × 364 × … × 343) ÷ 365²³ ≈ <strong>0.493</strong>\n\nSo the chance of at least one match is 1 − 0.493 ≈ <strong>50.7%</strong>."},
    {"heading": "The curve rises fast", "body": "With 10 people, the chance of a match is about 12%. With 23 it’s about 51%. With 30, about 71%. With 50, about 97%. With 70, about 99.9%.\n\nThe number of pairs grows roughly with the <strong>square</strong> of the group size, so match chances shoot up much faster than our linear intuition expects. In the 2014 World Cup, half of the 32 squads of 23 players had at least one shared birthday, almost exactly as predicted."},
    {"heading": "Hackers use it: the birthday attack", "body": "Computers fingerprint files and passwords with <strong>hash functions</strong>, which turn any data into a fixed-length code. A “collision” is when two different inputs give the same code, which can let attackers forge documents.\n\nThanks to the birthday effect, if a hash has N possible outputs, you only need to try around √N inputs to find a collision, not N. A 64-bit hash (about 1.8 × 10¹⁹ outputs) can be broken with only about 4 billion tries. That’s why modern hashes use 256 bits."},
    {"heading": "Coincidences are everywhere", "body": "The lesson generalizes. With many people and many possible coincidences, <em>some</em> surprising match is almost guaranteed, even though any <em>particular</em> one is unlikely. Dreaming of a friend the night before they call, or bumping into a neighbour on holiday abroad, are examples.\n\nStatisticians call this the <strong>law of truly large numbers</strong>: with a large enough number of opportunities, outrageous-seeming things happen all the time."}
  ],
  "diagram": {
    "after": 2,
    "svg": "<svg viewBox='0 0 400 215' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Chance of a shared birthday rises steeply with group size: 50 percent at 23 people, nearly 100 percent at 70'><line class='line' x1='40' y1='15' x2='40' y2='170' stroke-width='1.2'/><line class='line' x1='40' y1='170' x2='392' y2='170' stroke-width='1.2'/><g class='muted' stroke='none' font-size='12'><text x='34' y='24' text-anchor='end'>100%</text><text x='34' y='99' text-anchor='end'>50%</text><text x='34' y='174' text-anchor='end'>0%</text><text x='90' y='186' text-anchor='middle'>10</text><text x='190' y='186' text-anchor='middle'>30</text><text x='290' y='186' text-anchor='middle'>50</text><text x='390' y='186' text-anchor='end'>70 people</text></g><line class='muted' x1='40' y1='95' x2='392' y2='95' stroke-width='0.6' stroke-dasharray='3 4'/><line class='muted' x1='155' y1='95' x2='155' y2='170' stroke-width='0.6' stroke-dasharray='3 4'/><polyline class='accent-s' stroke-width='3.5' points='40,170 65,166 90,152.5 115,132 140,108.4 155,94 190,64.1 240,36.4 290,24.5 340,20.9 390,20.2'/><circle cx='155' cy='94' r='4.5' class='accent'/><text x='163' y='112' font-weight='700'>23 people → 50.7%</text><text class='muted' x='216' y='206' text-anchor='middle' stroke='none'>Chance that at least two people share a birthday</text></svg>",
    "caption": "The chance of at least one shared birthday climbs steeply, because the number of pairs grows much faster than the number of people."
  },
  "teachBack": [
    {"q": "Why do people overestimate how many people are needed for a shared birthday?", "model": "They think about someone matching their own birthday, which is unlikely. But any pair can match, and 23 people form 253 pairs, so the chances add up quickly."},
    {"q": "How do you calculate the probability for 23 people?", "model": "Compute the chance that nobody matches: 365/365 × 364/365 × … × 343/365 ≈ 0.493. Then subtract from 1 to get about 0.507, or 50.7%."},
    {"q": "How does the birthday paradox apply to computer security?", "model": "For a hash with N possible outputs, collisions become likely after only about √N tries, not N. So hashes need to be long. A 64-bit hash can be broken in about 4 billion tries, which is why 256-bit hashes are used."}
  ],
  "quiz": [
    {"q": "How many pairs of people are there in a group of 23?", "options": ["23", "46", "253", "529"], "answer": 2, "explain": ["That’s the number of people.", "That’s 23 × 2.", "Correct: 23 × 22 ÷ 2 = 253.", "That’s 23², which counts each pair twice and includes people paired with themselves."]},
    {"q": "What is the chance of at least one shared birthday among 23 people?", "options": ["About 6%", "About 23%", "About 51%", "About 99%"], "answer": 2, "explain": ["Far too low.", "Too low.", "Correct: about 50.7%.", "That needs about 57 people."]},
    {"q": "Why is it easier to calculate “no match” first?", "options": ["Because “no match” is always more likely", "Because it’s one simple chain of multiplications, instead of adding up many overlapping cases", "Because it avoids leap years", "It isn’t easier"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["Not true after 23 people.", "Correct. “At least one match” has many overlapping cases. Its complement is a single product.", "Leap years are a separate simplification.", "It’s much easier."]},
    {"q": "How many tries does a birthday attack need against a hash with N outputs?", "options": ["About N", "About N/2", "About √N", "About log N"], "answer": 2, "explain": ["That’s roughly what you’d need to hit one specific target value.", "Still too many.", "Correct. Collisions appear after about √N tries.", "Too few."]},
    {"q": "About how many people do you need for a 50% chance someone shares YOUR birthday?", "options": ["23", "183", "253", "365"], "answer": 2, "explain": ["That’s for any two people to match.", "A common guess, but too few.", "Correct. You need about 253 other people.", "Even 365 people wouldn’t guarantee a match with you."]}
  ],
  "goDeeper": [
    "Do real birthdays cluster (e.g. September in many countries)? How does that change the odds?",
    "How many people do you need for a 50% chance that three share a birthday? (About 88.)",
    "Read about the “law of truly large numbers” by Persi Diaconis and Frederick Mosteller."
  ]
},
{
  "id": "math-bayes-tests",
  "title": "Why a positive medical test might not mean you’re sick",
  "category": "math",
  "hook": "Suppose a test is 90% accurate, and you test positive for a rare disease. Most people, and in one famous study many doctors, guess you probably have it. The real answer can be under 10%. The tool that gets this right is Bayes’ theorem. It also applies to spam filters, court evidence, and any time you update a belief after new information.",
  "sections": [
    {"heading": "The setup", "body": "Imagine a disease that affects <strong>1%</strong> of people. A test for it has two numbers that describe it. <strong>Sensitivity: 90%</strong> means that if you have the disease, the test is positive 90% of the time. <strong>False positive rate: 9%</strong> means that if you’re healthy, the test still says positive 9% of the time.\n\nYou test positive. What’s the chance you actually have the disease?"},
    {"heading": "Think in people, not percentages", "body": "Picture <strong>1,000</strong> people. 1% have the disease: that’s <strong>10 people</strong>. The test catches 90% of them: 9 positives. The other <strong>990</strong> are healthy, but 9% of them test positive anyway: about <strong>89 false positives</strong>.\n\nSo there are 9 + 89 = <strong>98 positives</strong> in total, and only 9 of them are sick. That’s 9 ÷ 98 ≈ <strong>9%</strong>. A positive result raised your chance from 1% to 9%, which is a big jump, but you’re still most likely healthy."},
    {"heading": "Base rate neglect", "body": "The mistake is ignoring how rare the disease is to begin with, called the <strong>base rate</strong>. Healthy people vastly outnumber sick ones, so even a small false-positive rate produces more wrong positives than true ones.\n\nIn a well-known study by Gerd Gigerenzer, many doctors given a similar breast-screening problem guessed numbers like 70–90%. When the same problem was framed as counts of people, as above, far more of them got it right."},
    {"heading": "Bayes’ theorem in one line", "body": "Bayes’ theorem formalizes the counting:\n\nP(sick | positive) = P(positive | sick) × P(sick) ÷ P(positive)\n\nThe vertical bar means “given.” Plugging in: 0.9 × 0.01 ÷ 0.098 ≈ 0.092. The logic is simple: what you believed before (the <strong>prior</strong>), updated by how much more likely the evidence is if the belief is true."},
    {"heading": "What changes the answer", "body": "<strong>A higher prior</strong>: if you already have symptoms and, say, 20% of people like you have the disease, the same positive test means about <strong>71%</strong>. That’s why doctors test based on symptoms. <strong>A second test</strong>: if an independent retest is also positive, your 9% becomes the new prior, and the chance rises to roughly 50%. <strong>Fewer false positives</strong>: a more specific test dramatically raises the value of a positive result."}
  ],
  "diagram": {
    "after": 1,
    "svg": "<svg viewBox='0 0 400 225' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Tree of 1000 people: 10 sick of whom 9 test positive; 990 healthy of whom 89 test positive. 9 of 98 positives are sick'><rect x='155' y='8' width='90' height='30' rx='6' class='surface2'/><text x='200' y='28' text-anchor='middle' font-weight='700'>1,000 people</text><line class='line' x1='185' y1='38' x2='95' y2='72' stroke-width='1.5'/><line class='line' x1='215' y1='38' x2='305' y2='72' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='45' y='72' width='100' height='30' rx='6' class='surface2'/><text x='95' y='92' text-anchor='middle'>10 sick</text><rect x='255' y='72' width='100' height='30' rx='6' class='surface2'/><text x='305' y='92' text-anchor='middle'>990 healthy</text><line class='line' x1='80' y1='102' x2='45' y2='136' stroke-width='1.5'/><line class='line' x1='110' y1='102' x2='145' y2='136' stroke-width='1.5'/><line class='line' x1='290' y1='102' x2='255' y2='136' stroke-width='1.5'/><line class='line' x1='320' y1='102' x2='355' y2='136' stroke-width='1.5'/><rect x='10' y='136' width='75' height='30' rx='6' class='soft'/><text x='47' y='156' text-anchor='middle' font-weight='700'>9 +</text><text x='145' y='156' text-anchor='middle' class='muted' stroke='none'>1 −</text><rect x='215' y='136' width='80' height='30' rx='6' class='soft'/><text x='255' y='156' text-anchor='middle' font-weight='700'>89 +</text><text x='355' y='156' text-anchor='middle' class='muted' stroke='none'>901 −</text><text x='200' y='196' text-anchor='middle'>Positives: 9 + 89 = 98</text><text x='200' y='216' text-anchor='middle' font-weight='700'>Sick given positive: 9 ÷ 98 ≈ 9%</text></svg>",
    "caption": "Counting people makes the answer obvious: false positives from the large healthy group outnumber the true positives."
  },
  "teachBack": [
    {"q": "Explain, using 1,000 people, why a positive result here means only about a 9% chance of disease.", "model": "Of 1,000 people, 10 are sick and 9 of them test positive. Of the 990 healthy people, about 89 also test positive. So of 98 positives, only 9 are sick, which is about 9%."},
    {"q": "What is base rate neglect?", "model": "Ignoring how common something is to begin with. When a condition is rare, false positives from the huge healthy group can outnumber true positives, even with a fairly accurate test."},
    {"q": "Why does having symptoms change what a positive test means?", "model": "Symptoms raise the prior probability, the base rate among people like you. With a 20% prior, the same test’s positive result means about a 71% chance of disease instead of 9%."}
  ],
  "quiz": [
    {"q": "In the 1,000-person example, how many healthy people test positive?", "options": ["9", "About 89", "About 900", "0"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["That’s the number of sick people who test positive.", "Correct: 9% of 990 ≈ 89.", "That’s roughly the number of healthy people who correctly test negative.", "With a 9% false positive rate, some healthy people will test positive."]},
    {"q": "What does “sensitivity” mean for a test?", "options": ["How often it’s positive when you have the disease", "How often it’s negative when you’re healthy", "How expensive it is", "How fast it gives results"], "answer": 0, "explain": ["Correct. Here it’s 90%.", "That’s specificity.", "Not a statistical property.", "No."]},
    {"q": "If an independent retest is also positive, the chance of disease becomes roughly…", "options": ["Still 9%", "About 50%", "About 99%", "100%"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["The second result is new evidence, so the chance goes up.", "Correct. Starting from a 9% prior, the second positive pushes it to around 50%.", "Not that high with these test numbers.", "Tests are never perfectly certain."]},
    {"q": "Which would most improve how meaningful a positive result is?", "options": ["Testing only people with no symptoms", "Lowering the false positive rate", "Testing more healthy people", "Making the result arrive faster"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["That lowers the prior, making positives less meaningful.", "Correct. Fewer false positives means more of the positives are real.", "That adds more false positives.", "Speed doesn’t change accuracy."]},
    {"q": "In Bayes’ theorem, what is the “prior”?", "options": ["The test’s accuracy", "What you believed before seeing the new evidence", "The final answer", "The number of tests taken"], "answer": 1, "explain": ["That’s the likelihood part.", "Correct. Here it’s the 1% base rate.", "That’s the “posterior.”", "No."]}
  ],
  "goDeeper": [
    "What is the “prosecutor’s fallacy,” and how has it led to wrongful convictions?",
    "How do spam filters use Bayes’ theorem to judge emails word by word?",
    "Why do screening programs often use a cheap first test followed by a more specific second test?"
  ]
}
